A||||10||BillHarrison||Fiero Suspension/Brake Upgrade LONG!||||1||
Z||000000||BillHarrison||03-01-2001||07:24 AM||||Ok.  I am in the process of installing a 3.1 Turbo out of a 90 Turbo Grand Prix in my fiero.  It is an 86 GT.  Now, something is beginning to concern me.  Properly tuned, this engine should be capable of ~ 400 horsepower.  Now, a 400 horsepower chevette would scare me  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">  Turns out I have half of one under the front!  Not to mention the fact, I don't think the brakes will be adequit.  So, here are some thoughts, let's mull over them and see if they have been considered.  <P>1. GM is good about sharing parts/Mounting flanges, etc.  as we all know from the ability to swap about 500 different engine combos in and out of our cars  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> <P>2. After doing some research (I work for GM, that certainly helps  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">)  I found that a rear "HUB ASSEMBLY"  Off a 91-96 Grand prix will bolt onto the rear knuckle of our cars.  Now, with a caliper mounting adapter, that will give us 3 things.  A larger rotor.  A larger bolt pattern (which means ya cant keep the stock wheels).  And an ABS sensor.  Damn, we are halfway there.  <P>3. Now the front is where it gets tricky.  In my opinion, the most elegant solution would be to keep the UPPER/LOWER control arms, Then have a Knuckle machined that would bolt to the "STOCK" mounting points (Ball joints, and tie rods) BUT accept the FRONT hub assembly (3 bolt pattern common to most FWD gms) off the same 91-96 Grand prix.  Then we have LARGER brakes in the front, Yet again a larger bolt pattern, AND front ABS sensors.  <P>4. Now, for what its worth, the 91-96 W-body ABS system is very self contained, with the purchase of a controller, the pump, and a little wiring and hydraulic plumbing, VIOLA... ABS on your fiero.  And If your not interested in ABS.... Then DONT bother... you still get a HUGE brake upgrade. (Gotta recheck the specs, but off the top of my head...  12" or better in the front, and along the lines of 11 in the rear!)<P>Now of course, the only problem here is getting these CUSTOM hubs machined, but with some careful measurements, and a decent machine/Fabricating shop, this really should not be that difficult.  After all, some companys produce after market drop spindles, it would actually be possible to include a drop in the new design!  Kill 2 birds with 1 stone!<P>OK....  Now that is 1 mans insanity... anyone care to add to it?  Am I covering bases already attempted?  GOOD, BAD, Any comments, let me know!  I would love to have a suspension to handle the power I hope to be making! ||||reg||1
Z||000001||Carrolles||03-01-2001||08:37 AM||||Sounds very interesting.  Keep us posted.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Formula88||03-01-2001||08:47 AM||||Anything is possible with the proper amount of funding.  Whether or not your idea is practical will depend on the fabrication costs and the depth of your wallet.  It certainly sounds like a good idea.  Let me know how it turns out!<P>The one thing that does surprise me (or would surprise me) is getting that 400HP out of a 3.1 Turbo without it being rebuilt into a full-race engine.  I'd love to see it, though. ||||reg||6
Z||000003||mrfixit58||03-01-2001||08:49 AM||||I was right there with you until you said, "Now of course, the only problem here is getting these CUSTOM hubs machined, but with some careful measurements, and a decent machine/Fabricating shop, this really should not be that difficult. After all, some companys produce after market drop spindles, it would actually be possible to include a drop in the new design!"<P>Are you talking about machining a HUB or a SPINDLE? The spindle would be next to impossible without a casting process. The hub may be more do-able if you could be more specific on the requirements. You would probably have to develope a CAD drawing and get all the dimension. Armed with that, a good machinest on a C&C machine and/or lathe can build one.<P>I like people who think "out of the box".  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Keep going,<BR>Roy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||14
Z||000004||gabriel||03-01-2001||09:07 AM||||Ya lost me at ~400hp.<P>   Not saying it can't be done, but .....||||reg||1
Z||000005||Hym-The-Man||03-01-2001||09:45 AM||||I don't know if this will help, but you may try checking out this link: <A HREF="http://www.ipass.net/~fieroman/terranexus/fieroman/walt/walt.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.ipass.net/~fieroman/terranexus/fieroman/walt/walt.htm</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000006||Will||03-01-2001||10:57 AM||||Finally, someone who thinks like I do (almost)! And a GM engineer to boot!<P>1) Ponstiac 6000 front hub carriers are a bolt in swap for the Fiero rear hub carriers, and will accept Cadillac front wheel bearings with ABS sensors and 5x115 bolt circle. I don't know if the 6000 brakes can be used with the Cadillac bearings.<BR>Would probably not be a good idea to use GP front hubs in the front of our cars. I think that many GM bearing hubs require the axle end in them to hold them together and are damaged if loaded without it. Thanks for the info about the GP hubs in the rear, though.<P>2) Don't think "custom steering knuckle". Think "retrofit steering knuckle". I've been looking at the suspension geometries of a couple of cars. The 4th gen F-body steering knuckles will not work with Fiero suspension. The G-body steering knuckle probably will not work either (it's 3 inches taller ball joint to ball joint that the stock fiero spindle). I will be investigating 3rd gen F-body, C4, and possibly C5 Corvettes next week.<BR>Did 3rd gen F-bodies ever come with ABS?||||reg||1
Z||000007||ray b||03-01-2001||11:40 AM||||good idea ABS can save your life.front brakes need help even on my 88's.can other cars parts be reworked to fit, some hot rods use chevett based custom parts for front end.<BR>how different are stock chevett parts from our car's? <BR>S-10 truck parts??<BR>||||reg||3
Z||000008||lowCG||03-01-2001||12:05 PM||||Well I think that you could get some good torque from a setup like that,but the oiling system in those motors will haunt you eventually at higher boost levels.<BR>I know that the frontends have a longer history than the Chevette;starts with German Opel Ascona/Manta,goes to Isuzu,then Chevette.Lotus used German opel components through the seventies on the Esprit,wasn't all that bad.<BR>I think that the control arms are shorter on the later version like in our cars,but any one wanting to widen the stance might be able to use some Opel parts to do this.<BR>I'd think the Corvette parts would be a good place to start,aside from machining from scratch.<BR>Let us know how it looks if you try this.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000009||BillHarrison||03-01-2001||06:33 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by gabriel:<BR><B>Ya lost me at ~400hp.<P>   Not saying it can't be done, but .....</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Well...  Lets see.  Now granted, I have yet to mess with it much.  But stock = 205.  Now, this propels a 3800 pound Automatic Grand Prix to a 15.10.  Now, same Grand Prix's With CHIP ALONE have gone 13.7.  I'd Estimate that to be around 250.  WITH A CHIP.  Now, Getting 100 HP per liter with some mods (Turbo, Fuel, IC, exhaust) should not be diffucult AT all.  That becomes 310. And that should remain DEAD reliable.  Now, we just introduce our WIN LOSE equation here.  WIN extra horsepower with A little turn of the boost... LOSE engine longetevity.  Now granted, this is all in theory, BUT if my buddys GN can make 550 out of a 3.8, I dont think 400 out of a 3.1 is even close to impossible.  Of course, it seems downright easy seeing as how my RX-7 Turbo was dynoed at 375 RWHP, and that was from a 1.3  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">  After building that, 400 from a 3.1 should be CAKE ||||reg||1
Z||000010||gabriel||03-01-2001||07:17 PM||||Okay.<P>  When your'e done, post your Dyno slips and youv'e got a follower.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>  But then again, you'll have lots!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
